# In all environments, the following files are loaded if they exist,
# the latter taking precedence over the former:
#
#  * .env                contains default values for the environment variables needed by the app
#  * .env.local          uncommitted file with local overrides
#  * .env.$APP_ENV       committed environment-specific defaults
#  * .env.$APP_ENV.local uncommitted environment-specific overrides
#
# Real environment variables win over .env files.
#
# DO NOT DEFINE PRODUCTION SECRETS IN THIS FILE NOR IN ANY OTHER COMMITTED FILES.
# https://symfony.com/doc/current/configuration/secrets.html
#
# Run "composer dump-env prod" to compile .env files for production use (requires symfony/flex >=1.2).
# https://symfony.com/doc/current/best_practices.html#use-environment-variables-for-infrastructure-configuration

###> symfony/framework-bundle ###
APP_ENV=dev
# Placeholder on purpose: this file is public. Override in production via a real
# environment variable or the secrets vault.
APP_SECRET=dev-only-not-a-real-secret
APP_SHARE_DIR=var/share
###< symfony/framework-bundle ###

###> symfony/routing ###
# Configure how to generate URLs in non-HTTP contexts, such as CLI commands.
# See https://symfony.com/doc/current/routing.html#generating-urls-in-commands
DEFAULT_URI=http://localhost
###< symfony/routing ###

###> app/deployment ###
# **The domains this installation answers to** (XIV-93, docs/architecture.md
# §4.3), comma separated. Each entry admits the domain itself and every hostname
# under it, so `xivi.app` covers `xivi.app`, `acme.xivi.app` and
# `control.xivi.app` alike.
#
# **Empty — the default — means the Host header is not checked at all**, which
# is how this application has always behaved and is what a fresh checkout, the
# test suite and `bin/compose up` all need: development serves `*.localhost` and
# the suite invents a hostname per test.
#
# Set it on a real deployment. It is not a regular expression and must not be
# one: every dot in a hand-written `trusted_hosts` entry matches any character,
# which is the mistake §8.9 already declined to make for the control-plane
# firewall. Names, not patterns; the application writes the expressions.
#
# CONTROL_PLANE_HOST and SIGNUP_HOST are added for you, along with the loopback
# and container names in `app.system_hosts`, so the control plane cannot be
# locked out by a domain list that forgot it. What you have to get right is the
# domain your **customers** are served under — including, if self-service signup
# is on, the parent domain of SIGNUP_HOST, because that is where §8.14 puts a
# customer it provisions.
#
# What happens if it is wrong: a hostname outside the list gets a bare 400 and
# nothing else. `bin/console deploy:check-hosts` names every tenant that would
# get one, `bin/deploy` runs it before containers are replaced, and
# `tenant:provision` refuses to create a customer on a hostname this list would
# refuse.
XIVI_TRUSTED_DOMAINS=

# **Which addresses in front of this application may speak for the client**
# (XIV-93, §4.3), comma separated. Empty by default and empty is correct for the
# topology §4 describes — FrankenPHP terminates TLS itself, so nothing is in
# front of it and `X-Forwarded-*` is ignored, which is the safe default and also
# the reason the application sees the proxy's address rather than the client's
# when there *is* one.
#
# Set it to your load balancer's addresses if you have one; CIDR ranges work,
# and so do Symfony's shorthands `REMOTE_ADDR` and `private_ranges`. Which
# forwarded headers are then believed is decided in
# config/packages/framework.yaml and deliberately excludes X-Forwarded-Host —
# read the comment there before changing it, because tenant routing is the Host
# header.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# **The database role the customer-facing instance connects as** (XIV-143,
# docs/architecture/deployment.md §4.4), and the only reason to set it is so that a deploy
# can check its privileges.
#
# §4.4 splits a real deployment in two images against one control-plane
# database, and the sharp boundary between them is not the network: it is that
# the customer-facing instance's role holds `SELECT` on the registry tables and
# nothing else. `bin/console deploy:registry-grants` prints the SQL that arranges
# that, and the list of tables in it is derived from the mapping — so it grows
# whenever a release adds an entity under `App\Registry\Entity\`, and the grant
# only grows when somebody runs the command again.
#
# Name the role here and `bin/deploy` will check, on every release, that it holds
# exactly what this build needs: `bin/console deploy:check-grants`. What it
# catches is an upgrade whose grants were never re-run, which presents as
# `SQLSTATE[42501]: permission denied for table …` on every user's dashboard of
# every tenant — and it catches the opposite too, a role that has been given more
# than `SELECT` somewhere along the way.
#
# **Empty — the default — means no role is checked**, which is right for an
# installation served entirely by the internal image: one image, one database
# user, nothing to compare. It is a deployment fact rather than a secret, and the
# check reads the catalogue rather than connecting as the role, so nothing here
# needs the role's password.
XIVI_PUBLIC_ROLE=
###< app/deployment ###

###> app/monitoring ###
# **Where each scheduled job pings when it runs** (XIV-126,
# docs/architecture/deployment.md §4.5), as comma-separated `command=url` pairs.
#
# **Empty — the default — means no pings and nothing else changes.** Xivi does
# not require a monitoring service and never sends anything anywhere until this
# is set, which is the same promise XIVI_TRUSTED_DOMAINS above makes about the
# Host header.
#
# ## What this is for
#
# Nothing in Xivi notices when a scheduled job stops running. The screens built
# on those jobs are honest about staleness — a usage figure carries the moment it
# was taken, an uncollected customer reads *not collected yet* rather than zero —
# but that tells whoever looks, and nothing makes anybody look. A confirmed
# signup that never becomes a customer is a stopped `signup:provision`, and the
# first report of it arrives from the customer.
#
# An external monitor inverts that: the job pings when it finishes, and the
# **service** raises the alarm when a ping does not arrive. The thing watching is
# not the thing being watched, which is what a checker running inside this
# installation could never be.
#
# ## Setting it up
#
#   1. `bin/console deploy:crontab` prints the jobs this build needs, the cron
#      lines for them, and which of them are watched.
#   2. Create one check per job at your monitoring service and copy its ping URL.
#   3. List them here:
#
# XIVI_MONITOR_PINGS=signup:provision=https://hc-ping.com/<uuid>,tenant:usage:collect=https://hc-ping.com/<uuid>
#
# **Healthchecks is the recommendation and the reason is that you can run it
# yourself** — BSD-3-Clause, and a container beside this one is a perfectly good
# deployment of it. Better Stack's heartbeat URLs work here unchanged. Oh Dear
# and Cronitor spell the failure half differently and only the plain success ping
# will reach them; §4.5 compares all four.
#
# A URL that does not parse, names no command, or carries a query string is
# refused loudly on the next console command rather than skipped, because a
# skipped entry is a job nobody is watching on an installation that believes it
# configured watching.
XIVI_MONITOR_PINGS=
###< app/monitoring ###

###> doctrine/doctrine-bundle ###
# Format described at https://www.doctrine-project.org/projects/doctrine-dbal/en/latest/reference/configuration.html#connecting-using-a-url
# IMPORTANT: You MUST configure your server version, either here or in config/packages/doctrine.yaml
#
# DATABASE_URL="sqlite:///%kernel.project_dir%/var/data_%kernel.environment%.db"
# DATABASE_URL="mysql://app:!ChangeMe!@127.0.0.1:3306/app?serverVersion=8.0.32&charset=utf8mb4"
# DATABASE_URL="mysql://app:!ChangeMe!@127.0.0.1:3306/app?serverVersion=10.11.2-MariaDB&charset=utf8mb4"
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://app:!ChangeMe!@database:5432/app?serverVersion=18&charset=utf8"
###< doctrine/doctrine-bundle ###

###> app/tenancy ###
# Template for tenant DSNs created by "bin/console tenant:provision": {database}
# and {user} are replaced with the tenant's own database and role, and the
# password is generated per tenant and stored encrypted, never in this file.
# Per-tenant DSNs live in the control-plane database — never in per-domain env
# files (docs/architecture/deployment.md §4). Pass --dsn for a tenant on a different server.
TENANT_DSN_TEMPLATE="postgresql://{user}@database:5432/{database}?serverVersion=18&charset=utf8"

# Used only by provisioning and removal: CREATE DATABASE, CREATE ROLE, and — since
# XIV-94 — pg_terminate_backend, because "tenant:deprovision" disconnects whatever
# is attached before it drops. Ideally it should not be present in the environment
# of the web processes at all.
#
# **CREATEDB plus CREATEROLE is not enough on Postgres 16 and later**, which is
# what the advice here used to say. Measured on Postgres 18: such a role cannot
# terminate a session belonging to a tenant role it created itself (42501), cannot
# CREATE DATABASE ... OWNER that role ("must be able to SET ROLE"), and cannot drop
# the resulting database ("must be owner of database") — all because a CREATEROLE
# grant now carries ADMIN without INHERIT or SET. A narrowed role therefore needs
# GRANT pg_signal_backend plus a GRANT ... WITH SET TRUE, INHERIT TRUE on every
# tenant role; until that is designed (§4.1 leaves it open), a superuser is what
# this is. Development and test use one.
TENANT_ADMIN_DSN="postgresql://app:!ChangeMe!@database:5432/postgres?serverVersion=18&charset=utf8"

# **The hostname the control plane is served on** (XIV-57). Setting this is a
# deployment step, and the only one this feature has: the value becomes an entry
# in `app.system_hosts`, so the host that serves the control plane is by
# construction one that resolves no tenant, and it is what the control-plane
# firewall matches on. See docs/architecture/identity-and-access.md §8.9 and DEPLOYMENT.md.
#
# It must be a name no customer of this installation is served on. Provisioning
# refuses to route a tenant here, so the mistake fails at `tenant:provision`
# rather than at the moment somebody discovers their customer's login page has
# turned into ours.
#
# `.localhost` by default because browsers resolve those to the loopback with no
# hosts file, so a fresh checkout can reach https://control.localhost/control/
# without configuring anything. A real deployment names a real hostname here —
# and, given what is behind it, ideally one that is not guessable from the
# customer-facing domain.
CONTROL_PLANE_HOST=control.localhost

# **Which addresses may reach the control plane at all** (XIV-124,
# docs/architecture/identity-and-access.md §8.9), comma separated. Addresses or CIDR ranges, IPv4 or
# IPv6, mixed freely:
#
#   CONTROL_PLANE_ALLOWED_IPS=198.51.100.7,203.0.113.0/24,2001:db8:1::/48
#
# **Empty — the default — means no restriction**, which is how this application
# has always behaved and is what a fresh checkout, the suite and `bin/compose up`
# all need. Setting it changes nothing for customers: it applies only to requests
# arriving on CONTROL_PLANE_HOST.
#
# This is defence in depth and not a replacement for anything. The sign-in, the
# operator-only user provider and ROLE_OPERATOR are what keep people out and all
# of them still hold; what this adds is a layer in *front* of them, so that an
# operator's console is not a password prompt the whole internet may attempt.
# It is worth setting precisely because the control-plane hostname is not a
# boundary — anybody who can set the `Host` header reaches the sign-in page.
#
# The address is resolved through TRUSTED_PROXIES and the forwarded headers
# framework.yaml believes, never from a raw header, so **a load balancer in front
# of this application means TRUSTED_PROXIES has to be set too** — otherwise every
# request looks like it came from the balancer and this list has to hold the
# balancer, which admits everybody behind it.
#
# **You can lock yourself out with this**, and unlike a wrong hostname no
# customer will notice for you. Run `bin/console deploy:check-control-plane` —
# with `--address=…` to ask about one in particular — before you depend on it.
# A refused request is an empty 403 and one `error` line naming the address, the
# variable and what it admits. A bad entry does not switch the restriction off;
# it simply admits nobody.
CONTROL_PLANE_ALLOWED_IPS=

# Keys that encrypt tenant database passwords at rest, as {id: base64 32 bytes}.
# Dev values only: in production use the secrets vault
# ("bin/console secrets:set TENANT_SECRET_KEYS") or a KMS-injected variable.
# Generate a key: php -r "echo base64_encode(random_bytes(32));"
#
# Rotation: add a second key here, point TENANT_SECRET_KEY_ID at it, run
# "bin/console tenant:rotate-secrets", then remove the old key once that command
# reports nothing stale. Stored values name the key they were written with, so
# both keys work during the changeover and the job can be interrupted.
TENANT_SECRET_KEYS='{"dev":"ZGV2LW9ubHkta2V5LWRvLW5vdC11c2UtaW4tcHJvZCE="}'
TENANT_SECRET_KEY_ID=dev
###< app/tenancy ###

###> sensiolabs/gotenberg-bundle ###
# The document converter (XIV-4). A service on the compose network, never
# published: it takes a .docx and gives back a PDF, and holds nothing in between.
GOTENBERG_URL=http://gotenberg:3000
###< sensiolabs/gotenberg-bundle ###

###> xivi/brand ###
# The mark shown in the top bar and on the login page, as a file name inside
# assets/brand/ (XIV-48). That directory is gitignored: a logo is supplied by the
# deployment and never committed. Empty — the default — falls back to the
# installation's name in text, which is what a clean checkout and CI do.
#
# Point XIVI_BRAND_DIR at a directory to keep the file outside the project; see
# assets/brand/README.md.
APP_LOGO=
###< xivi/brand ###

###> symfony/mailer ###
# Where mail goes. The default is nowhere, on purpose: a deployment that has not
# said where to send mail should drop it rather than guess at a local sendmail,
# and this file is the one every environment starts from. Dev overrides it in
# .env.dev to point at the catcher (XIV-41); a real deployment names its own
# server in a real environment variable.
#
# Note what this is not. Dropping mail by default is a *default*, not a
# guarantee — anything that sets this variable is believed. That guarantee is now
# App\Mail\NonProductionMailGuard (XIV-37), which refuses to *build* a delivering
# transport outside production whatever this says, so a value set by accident in
# dev or test fails loudly instead of sending.
MAILER_DSN=null://null

# The address this instance sends as for a customer who has not named one of
# their own (XIV-37, docs/architecture/identity-and-access.md §8.7). Their name still appears on the
# mail and their address becomes the Reply-To; only the envelope is ours, because
# our domain is not entitled to claim theirs and SPF exists to say so.
#
# Empty is allowed and is the default: mail then comes from no-reply@ at the
# hostname that customer reaches this installation on, which is this instance's
# domain for them. Set it to something a person can receive at if that is not
# what you want.
MAILER_SENDER=

# The largest document this installation will attach to an email, in bytes
# (XIV-40, docs/architecture/data-model.md §5.15). Seven mebibytes by default: base64 makes
# an attachment a third bigger on the wire, so this arrives as a message of about
# nine and a half — inside the 10 MB that is the most common conservative limit
# among *receiving* servers, and Postfix's own default. Anything larger is
# refused on the screen with the size and the ceiling in the sentence, because
# the alternative is a bounce hours later that nobody reads.
#
# Raise it if you know your relay and everybody you write to takes more; lower it
# if yours takes less. The number that matters is theirs, not ours.
XIVI_MAX_ATTACHMENT_BYTES=7340032
###< symfony/mailer ###

###> app/signup ###
# **The hostname the public signup endpoint is served on, and whether it exists
# at all** (XIV-64, docs/architecture/identity-and-access.md §8.12).
#
# Empty — the default — means **no route is registered**. Not a route that
# answers 404: the routing table has nothing in it, so there is no controller for
# a listener, an access rule or a bug to fail to keep away from. A hostname means
# the endpoint is served there and on no other host.
#
# One variable rather than a flag beside a hostname, because two facts can
# disagree and the disagreement everybody eventually has is "enabled, but nobody
# said where".
#
# **It must not be CONTROL_PLANE_HOST.** That host answers to people who can see
# every customer, and §8.9 asks for it to be hard to guess; this one is
# configured into a third party's marketing site, which is the opposite kind of
# secret. The application refuses to build a routing table when the two are
# equal.
#
# Like CONTROL_PLANE_HOST it becomes an entry in `app.system_hosts`, so a request
# here resolves no tenant — which it has to, since a signup is about a customer
# who does not exist yet. Set it in lowercase: that list is compared literally.
#
# **Setting this is not the whole deployment step** (XIV-98, §8.14). The endpoint
# records signups and provisions nothing at all; what turns a confirmed one into
# a customer is `bin/console signup:provision`, and it needs a line in cron.
# Without it a confirmed signup sits in the intake table and the person who made
# it waits for a mail that never comes. README has the crontab line.
#
# This host also decides where customers live: a self-service tenant is served at
# its own name under **this host's parent domain**, so signup at
# `signup.xivi.app` puts customers at `acme.xivi.app`. That is the address the
# form shows them before they submit.
SIGNUP_HOST=

# **Whether this installation draws its own landing page and signup form**
# (XIV-65, docs/architecture/identity-and-access.md §8.13).
#
# The second switch, and it composes with the one above rather than repeating it:
#
#   SIGNUP_HOST empty            neither. No route is registered for the page or
#                                the endpoint, whatever this says. **This is the
#                                default, and it is the right one for a single
#                                company self-hosting** — an open endpoint that
#                                records signups is a liability to them rather
#                                than a feature.
#   SIGNUP_HOST set, this false  the endpoint only. For a deployment that has
#                                built its own site and posts to the published
#                                contract; the built-in page would be a second,
#                                worse front door onto the same intake.
#   SIGNUP_HOST set, this true   the page and the endpoint. What the company
#                                selling this runs.
#
# **Off means the routes do not exist**, the same way it does above: the page's
# three routes are absent from the compiled matcher and from `debug:router`
# rather than answering 404. A page that is switched off but whose actions still
# run is exactly what SignupRouteLoader was written to make impossible.
#
# The page is served on SIGNUP_HOST — at `/`, with the intake under
# `/api/signup/v1/` and the confirmation link under `/signup/confirm/`. One
# hostname from the form to the mailbox and back, because a visitor asked to
# confirm at a name they have never seen has been handed the shape of a phishing
# mail. A deployment that wants the form under its own marketing domain does not
# set a variable here: it puts its own page there, which is the middle state.
SIGNUP_PAGE=true

# **The shared secret the calling site presents**, in the `X-Xivi-Signup-Key`
# header. Compared in constant time, and an empty value refuses everybody rather
# than admitting them: a deployment that set SIGNUP_HOST and forgot this has
# published an anonymous endpoint, and a feature that does not work is noticed in
# minutes while a feature that works for everybody is not noticed at all.
#
# Generate one: php -r "echo bin2hex(random_bytes(32));"
# In production use the secrets vault or a real environment variable, never this
# file. Rotating it is editing one value here and one on the calling site.
XIVI_SIGNUP_SECRET=

# **Which plans self-service may ask for**, comma separated, most ordinary first
# — a request that names no plan gets the first one. `Tenant::$plan` has existed
# since the registry did; billing has not been built, and the intake still
# refuses to pretend one plan is the only one, because that is the assumption
# that would have to be undone first. A plan not in this list is refused at the
# endpoint rather than stored.
XIVI_SIGNUP_PLANS=standard

# **Who a confirmation comes from.** MAILER_SENDER, exactly as for a tenant's
# mail — and an empty one falls back the same way §8.7 falls back, one noun
# along: `no-reply@` at SIGNUP_HOST rather than at a customer's primary domain.
# That is honest for the same reason, since SIGNUP_HOST is the name this
# prospective customer's site just posted to and the name their confirmation link
# points at. Nothing extra to set.
###< app/signup ###

###> app/pricing ###
# **The currency this deployment's module price list is in** (XIV-101), as an ISO
# 4217 code — CHF, EUR, GBP. One answer for the whole installation.
#
# **Not the currency a customer works in.** That one is on their own profile
# (§8.6) and is about the invoices *they* write. This is what the company running
# Xivi charges for a module, and a customer whose profile says EUR does not
# change what this deployment charges.
#
# **The prices themselves are not here and must not come here.** They live on the
# control-plane `module` row and an operator sets them at `/control/modules`,
# because a price somebody has to edit in a file is a price nobody can change
# without a deploy. The currency is the opposite kind of fact: a deployment picks
# it once when it is installed, and changing it invalidates every figure on the
# list at once — 49.00 CHF and 49.00 EUR are not the same offer — so it is a
# re-pricing exercise rather than an edit, and needing a deploy for it is the
# right amount of friction.
#
# Empty is a real answer and is the shipped default: prices then render as bare
# numbers and the operator screen names this variable. A guessed currency is
# wrong quietly, which is §8.6's argument for the same emptiness one level down.
PRICE_CURRENCY=
###< app/pricing ###
